WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:06.060
It's no secret that Hollywood has a habit&nbsp;
of playing fast and loose with the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.380
in the name of entertainment, and we all&nbsp;
know that the words 'based on real events'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.880
are really code for 'we made 90% of the&nbsp;
plot up to make it more interesting.'

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.400
There's a long list of movies based on so-called&nbsp;
'true stories' that have been significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.560
spiced up for the silver screen, from Catch&nbsp;
Me If You Can to The Texas Chainsaw Massacre.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.800
The Revenant starring Leonardo DiCaprio&nbsp;
is another good example. Released in 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.140
to critical acclaim, the film tells the&nbsp;
story of American frontiersman Hugh Glass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.040
who gets horrifically mauled by&nbsp;
a grizzly bear before going on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.720
epic journey to wreak his revenge on the&nbsp;
men who abandoned him in the aftermath.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:51.960
It sounds like textbook Hollywood hyperbole,&nbsp;
and the Revenant certainly took some serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.860
liberties with the truth. In fact,&nbsp;
outside of the bear-mauling itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.840
almost every major plot point in&nbsp;
the entire film is utter nonsense.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.080
But here's the funny thing. While it's&nbsp;
absolutely true that Hollywood had its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.400
wicked way with Hugh Glass' story -&nbsp;
if anything, the fictional retelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.460
was actually significantly *less* epic&nbsp;
than the real events of his life. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.440
they didn't even mention the part where he was&nbsp;
forced to become a pirate, or the bit where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.280
watched his friend burned alive with slivers of&nbsp;
superheated pine. And no, I'm making that up.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.420
This is the story of Hugh Glass - a tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.280
so incredible Hollywood toned it&nbsp;
down to make it more believable.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.160
We don’t know a whole heap about Hugh’s early&nbsp;
life, but he was probably born sometime around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:30.540
1783 near Philadelphia. These days we know him as&nbsp;
a famous frontiersman and bare-knuckled boxer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.960
bears, but when he first pinged into existence&nbsp;
on history's radar, he was actually a sailor.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.440
His ship was attacked by pirates somewhere off&nbsp;
the coast of what is now Texas in 1819. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.640
the crew were killed, but after acquitting&nbsp;
himself particularly well in the fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:54.480
Glass was given a choice - ally with the pirates,&nbsp;
or join the rest of his crew leaking blood and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.600
guts onto the deck. After deliberating for&nbsp;
several microseconds, Glass chose the former.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:07.680
As is the case with most bold career changes,&nbsp;
becoming a pirate wasn't easy. For one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.300
there was a lot more raping and pillaging than&nbsp;
Glass was used to, and despite having a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.160
work ethic he never really took to it. At what&nbsp;
I like to think of as the equivalent of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:25.560
first annual appraisal, Glass' new boss - the&nbsp;
notorious pirate Jean Lafitte - was not pleased.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.540
As punishment for his perceived crimes,&nbsp;
Glass was set to stand trial before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.540
Lafitte beside another condemned crew member.
Both men knew their fate would be a long walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:42.720
from a short plank, so they made a run for&nbsp;
it (which quickly became a swim for it).

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.620
They were 2 miles out to sea at the time, but&nbsp;
somehow both made it to shore alive, where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:56.020
set about living off the land, avoiding search&nbsp;
parties, and trying not get eaten by cannibals -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.080
they were in Karankawa territory, a Native&nbsp;
American tribe well known for their predilection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.960
for human flesh. I should point out that there’s&nbsp;
no evidence the Karankawa were cannibals - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.360
rumour seems to have been started by Spanish&nbsp;
colonists, and it stuck. Still, Glass and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.160
buddy didn't know that, and quite understandably&nbsp;
they had no intention of putting it to the test.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:24.000
Keeping a low profile, the two men headed north&nbsp;
in search of friendly settlements. 700 miles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.900
later in what is now Kansas, they were captured by&nbsp;
members of the Pawnee tribe. On the bright side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:38.520
the Pawnee weren't cannibals - on the shite side&nbsp;
- they *did* practise ritual human sacrifice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.880
Soon after they were caught, Glass' travelling&nbsp;
companion was stripped naked and burnt at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.700
stake. As he died, his writhing body was stabbed&nbsp;
repeatedly with burning splinters of pinewood.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.800
Glass would have been next, but as the&nbsp;
tribesmen began to prepare him for the ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:01.200
he reached into his pocket and produced&nbsp;
a vial of a red ore called cinnabar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.740
which he presented to the tribe's chief with&nbsp;
a deep bow, and awaited his fate with dignity.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:14.460
Cinnabar was highly prized by the Pawnee - and the&nbsp;
chief was so impressed with the gift - as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:20.280
the gravity and respect with which it had been&nbsp;
given - that not only did he spare Glass' life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.140
he accepted him into the tribe. It's thought&nbsp;
that Glass went on to live with the Pawnee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.220
for several years, and there's a pretty&nbsp;
good chance his later feats of survival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.600
were made possible at least in part&nbsp;
by the things he learned from them.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.580
He left the tribe sometime in the early 1820s,&nbsp;
making his way to St. Louis, where he responded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:50.100
to an advert to join the recently-formed (and&nbsp;
soon to be world famous) Rocky Mountain Fur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:57.180
Company. The company, founded by William Ashley&nbsp;
and Andrew Henry, was seeking tough men to join a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.400
2000 mile expedition up the Missouri River to&nbsp;
the recently established Fort Henry, which was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.620
serve as a base for fur trapping both on the river&nbsp;
itself and in the Rocky Mountains to the west.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.900
The men who joined that now legendary&nbsp;
expedition would come to be known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.100
Ashley's 100, and they were some of&nbsp;
the toughest men ever to have lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:22.260
including some now-household names&nbsp;
like Jedediah Smith and Jim Bridger.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:28.620
Progress up the river in two large boats was&nbsp;
slow but steady until an attack by members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:34.380
the Arikara tribe left 14 of Glass' new&nbsp;
colleagues dead and many more injured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.960
Glass himself was shot in the leg during the&nbsp;
skirmish, and the men were forced back downriver.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:45.900
Founders, William Ashley and Andrew Henry had&nbsp;
sunk a huge amount of capital into the expedition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:52.620
and returning home empty handed simply wasn't an&nbsp;
option if the fledgling company was to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:58.560
With the Missouri deemed too dangerous, the&nbsp;
group split into two - one, led by Ashley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.260
went west in search of beaver and immediate&nbsp;
profits, while the other, led by Henry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:10.200
including Hugh Glass, went north to Fort Henry&nbsp;
on foot in the hope of bypassing the Arikara.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.820
The plan was only partially successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:20.400
and the group ended up in several more minor&nbsp;
skirmishes with the locals. But for Glass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.680
the biggest danger wouldn't come from&nbsp;
muskets and tomahawks, but teeth and claws.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:30.480
He was out hunting one day when he heard&nbsp;
a sudden commotion in the undergrowth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.620
He turned to see a massive shape&nbsp;
hurtling towards him at full speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:40.080
and he had just enough time to raise his&nbsp;
rifle and fire before the creature was on him.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.880
If you've seen the Revenant, you'll&nbsp;
know exactly what happens next - Glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.980
had inadvertently disturbed a mother&nbsp;
grizzly bear and her cubs. Grizzlies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.960
aren’t exactly friendly at the best of times,&nbsp;
but a mother with her young is the dictionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.300
definition of overprotective. Glass was&nbsp;
mauled to within an inch of his life.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.400
The way the story's usually told, he managed&nbsp;
to stab the bear to death during the struggle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:10.620
but the truth is, an adult grizzly in full-on&nbsp;
attack mode is practically invincible once it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.120
gets up close, and chances are it&nbsp;
was Glass' companions - alerted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.960
his screams - who brought the bear&nbsp;
down with sustained musket fire.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.980
To begin with, they thought Glass was dead&nbsp;
too, until someone noticed his chest was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:31.620
still rising and falling. Amazingly, death was&nbsp;
not yet calling, but one of his legs was broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:36.480
and his back was so badly lacerated that his&nbsp;
ribs were visible beneath his bloodied skin.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:41.820
It was obvious to everyone that Glass wouldn't&nbsp;
last. So they made him as comfortable as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.300
could, strapped him to a makeshift&nbsp;
stretcher, and continued on their way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:50.580
They didn't get far - Glass was a dead&nbsp;
weight, severely hampering their pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.560
in this treacherous place - they were&nbsp;
still in Arikara territory after all.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.740
With little other choice, Henry offered a&nbsp;
reward of $80 - the equivalent of a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.340
of grand in today's money - to any&nbsp;
men who would stay behind with Glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:10.740
in his final hours to give him a proper&nbsp;
Christian burial. Two men stepped forward.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:16.260
The first was a veteran trapper named John&nbsp;
Fitzgerald, and the second was a 17-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:22.260
old rookie identified only as 'Bridger' - some&nbsp;
historians believe this was probably Jim Bridger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:27.000
who would go on to become one of the most&nbsp;
famous frontiersmen of the Old American West.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.780
Fitzgerald and Bridger no doubt assumed they&nbsp;
were going to make some easy cash - Glass was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:39.120
at death's door, with the only sign he hadn't&nbsp;
already been invited inside for tea and hobknobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:44.940
with the Grim Reaper himself being the occasional&nbsp;
flicker of his eyes. But day after day went by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:49.680
and while Glass certainly wasn't getting&nbsp;
any better, he wasn't dead either.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.840
Fitzgerald and Bridger were getting twitchy.&nbsp;
Nobody had expected Glass to live this long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:59.160
and $80 was beginning to look like&nbsp;
inadequate compensation for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.920
dangerous task. After 5 days went&nbsp;
by with no sign of Glass expiring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:11.880
they decided to act. They took his rifle,&nbsp;
knife, and flint, and left him for dead.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.160
The two men reached Fort Henry just a couple&nbsp;
of days after the rest of the group and duly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:23.280
confirmed that Glass was dead and buried, safe&nbsp;
in the knowledge that there was no way he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.620
possibly come back and prove them wrong (I'm&nbsp;
guessing they'd never seen the Lion King).

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:37.620
Glass, meanwhile, had woken from his stupor, still&nbsp;
alive but in Dire Straits. He managed to splint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:42.840
his broken leg, but the rest of his injuries were&nbsp;
beginning to fester and he had no supplies or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:48.780
survival gear. Pretty much the only thing he *did*&nbsp;
have was a burning desire to get his revenge on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:54.240
the men who'd robbed him and left him to die alone&nbsp;
in the wilderness hundreds of miles from home.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:58.740
They say that the journey of a thousand&nbsp;
miles starts with a single step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:04.860
but that assumes one can actually&nbsp;
walk. Hugh Glass' long road back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:10.620
civilization most likely started with a&nbsp;
3 inch scrabble and a very loud scream.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:16.500
Most people would have given up there&nbsp;
and then. He was 300 miles from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:21.480
nearest friendly settlement - a small trading&nbsp;
post called Fort Kiowa - and on the journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:27.420
there was a whole host of hazards. But&nbsp;
Hugh Glass was not 'most people.' He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:33.300
an ex-pirate who'd survived being shot in the&nbsp;
leg and mauled by a bear, and who'd learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.440
survive in the wilds of North America from&nbsp;
the people who'd lived there for millennia.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:46.680
Day by day, he dragged himself onwards, drinking&nbsp;
water from streams and mashing passing insects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:53.400
into his mouth for nourishment. After about a week&nbsp;
of this pitiful existence he stumbled - across a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:58.920
pack of wolves feasting on a buffalo calf. He&nbsp;
waited patiently for the predators to eat their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:05.880
fill, then dragged himself over to the carcass.&nbsp;
It was a find that very probably saved his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:12.060
He set up a makeshift camp nearby and gorged&nbsp;
on buffalo meat until his strength returned.&nbsp;

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:17.340
As he slowly recuperated, he allowed&nbsp;
maggots to proliferate in the worst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:20.760
his wounds. That might sound&nbsp;
like a truly terrible idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:26.580
but maggot therapy is actually an FDA approved&nbsp;
biotreatment for various ailments even today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:34.080
In Glass' case, the writhing yellow larvae ate&nbsp;
away his rotting flesh, preventing gangrene.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.340
He was still badly wounded, but when&nbsp;
he set out again for Fort Kiowa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:44.460
he was able to move far more freely. When&nbsp;
he hit the Missouri River soon afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:50.340
he either borrowed a skin boat from a friendly&nbsp;
tribe of Lakota, or possibly fashioned his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:54.840
depending on which source you read. Either way,&nbsp;
the next leg of his journey was significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:58.920
smoother than the first - he simply floated&nbsp;
down river all the way to Fort Kiowa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:05.220
arriving approximately 6 weeks after his&nbsp;
date with destiny at the paws of the grizzly.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:12.360
After this epic journey back to civilization&nbsp;
and safety, you might think Hugh Glass would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:17.520
have put his feet up for a bit. But while&nbsp;
most of his physical injuries had healed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:22.920
the mental ones had not. The men who had&nbsp;
abandoned him to die were still out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:29.460
somewhere living their lives as if nothing had&nbsp;
happened. Glass was determined to put that right.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.420
After just a couple of days at the fort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:38.400
he heard about a new expedition heading back&nbsp;
up the Missouri in the direction of Fort Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:44.400
where he assumed Fitzgerald and Bridger would&nbsp;
be waiting. Glass signed up immediately.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.900
It turned out to be an eventful trip. The&nbsp;
expedition was only due to go as far as a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:54.180
of villages about halfway between Fort Kiowa and&nbsp;
Fort Henry, and so Glass planned to hike the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.180 --> 00:15:00.420
of the way on his own. The day after he split with&nbsp;
the other members of the group, they were ambushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:07.140
by Arikara and every single one of them was&nbsp;
slaughtered. Glass had a run-in with the Arikara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:13.140
himself, only narrowly escaping with his life&nbsp;
after warriors from a rival tribe came to his aid.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:19.200
When he finally arrived at Fort Henry after 6&nbsp;
weeks on the river and a month of solo hiking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:26.340
instead of an epic showdown with the men who'd&nbsp;
wronged him, he found... absolutely nothing. Fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:32.700
Henry had been abandoned, and, according to a note&nbsp;
he found pasted to the door, relocated nearby.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:39.900
In the end, he strode into Fort Henry 2.0&nbsp;
more than 4 months after he'd been mauled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:44.220
by the grizzly. The men stationed at the&nbsp;
fort must have thought they were seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:49.440
a ghost - after all, they had it on good&nbsp;
authority that Glass was dead and buried.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:55.980
Not our friend Bridger though - he knew exactly&nbsp;
what he was seeing: a big fucking problem.

00:15:56.700 --> 00:16:01.020
I think everyone can relate to that horrible&nbsp;
sinking feeling you get when someone catches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:08.640
you in a lie. I’m guessing Bridger felt it too,&nbsp;
only worse - because *his* lie was also pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:14.340
a gun in his face. In the end, he needn’t&nbsp;
have worried. Glass was out for revenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:19.920
but he was also a reasonable man. Bridger&nbsp;
seemed to be genuinely remorseful for his crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:25.560
and, after pleading his case to Glass, the&nbsp;
grizzlied frontiersman ultimately decided that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.760
as the older man, the real&nbsp;
blame lay with Fitzgerald.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:34.200
Unfortunately, Fitzgerald had left&nbsp;
the camp more than a month earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:37.740
to join up with the army at Fort&nbsp;
Atkinson in what is now Nebraska.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:46.680
By this point winter had set in, and it was a full&nbsp;
two months before Glass could set off in pursuit.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:55.080
After another long hike and yet another deadly&nbsp;
run-in with the Arikara, Glass finally made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.080 --> 00:17:02.520
to Fort Atkinson, where he found himself&nbsp;
face to face with John Fitzgerald. Glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:09.180
was a simple man. He didn't embark on some long&nbsp;
speech or ask Fitzgerald to explain his actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:16.080
He simply pulled out his gun, levelled it at&nbsp;
Fitzgerald's head, and... did absolutely nothing.

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:20.520
Just at the moment he'd been about to pull&nbsp;
the trigger, a nearby army officer pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:25.260
out that murdering a member of the American&nbsp;
military in cold blood was a capital offence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:28.560
In other words, if Glass shot&nbsp;
Fitzgerald, he'd be hanged immediately.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.820
It must have taken a huge amount&nbsp;
of willpower to lower his weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:42.060
but lower it he did. He left Fitzgerald&nbsp;
with a parting promise - if he ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:47.220
left the protection of the army, Glass&nbsp;
would find him, and he would kill him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:53.460
Because he had a very particular set of skills,&nbsp;
skills he’d acquired over a very long career.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:01.380
Glass would live on the American frontier for&nbsp;
the rest of his life, trapping, trading, hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:06.660
and exploring. He was a veteran of many battles&nbsp;
with the various tribes whose lands the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:14.760
States of America was slowly claiming for its&nbsp;
own, but it was in 1833 at the approximate age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:21.840
of 50 that his luck finally ran out. He and two&nbsp;
fellow trappers were ambushed by a large party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:28.980
of Arikara braves while crossing a frozen river.&nbsp;
They were all shot, robbed, and finally scalped.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:36.060
In the years since, Hugh Glass' remarkable story&nbsp;
has quite rightly achieved legendary status.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:43.440
But as with many legendary tales, there's a fairly&nbsp;
good chance some of this one isn't true. Much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:48.720
what we know about Glass' life comes from sources&nbsp;
that are hard to trust entirely, and rumour has it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:54.180
Glass himself was fond of telling tall tales,&nbsp;
particularly when they concerned himself.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:00.420
Exactly where we should draw the line between&nbsp;
fact and fable is up for debate, but one thing's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:04.860
for certain: even if only one tenth of the&nbsp;
stories told about Hugh Glass are true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:12.360
he was undeniably one of the most remarkable men&nbsp;
- and greatest survivors - ever to have lived.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:13.695
Thanks for watching.

